# 🎫 Smart QueueLess Counter

**AI-powered digital queue management system** built with Python and Streamlit.
Customers take a digital token instead of standing in a line, staff manage counters from a control panel, and a machine-learning model predicts the waiting time.

---

## ✨ Features

| Module | What it does |
|---|---|
| 🏠 **Dashboard** | Live view of people waiting, estimated wait, open counters, tokens served today, the "Now Serving" token and the queue board |
| 🎫 **Get Token** | Choose a service (Banking, Hospital, College Office, etc.) and receive a digital token with a predicted wait time |
| 📷 **AI Queue Vision** | Upload an image or use the webcam to estimate how many people are in a queue (OpenCV demo) |
| 🧑‍💼 **Staff Console** | Open or close 4 counters, call the next token and mark tokens as completed |
| 📊 **Analytics** | Total tokens, served count, average wait, service usage chart, data table and CSV export |
| ⚙️ **System** | Technology stack overview |

## 🧠 How the wait-time prediction works

A `RandomForestRegressor` (scikit-learn) is trained at startup on synthetic data. It uses:

- queue size
- number of open counters
- average service time
- peak-hour factor

The prediction is cached with `st.cache_resource`, so training happens only once per server run.

> **Note:** the model is trained on simulated data. For production use, retrain it with real service-time logs from the `tokens` table.

## 🛠️ Tech Stack

- **Python** 3.11+
- **Streamlit**: web interface
- **SQLAlchemy + SQLite**: token storage (`queueless.db`, created automatically)
- **scikit-learn**: wait-time prediction
- **OpenCV + Pillow**: queue image analysis
- **Pandas / NumPy**: data processing

## 🚀 Getting Started

```bash
# 1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/<your-username>/<your-repo>.git
cd <your-repo>

# 2. (Recommended) create a virtual environment
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate        # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate

# 3. Install dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 4. Run the app
streamlit run app.py
```

The app opens at **http://localhost:8501**.

## 📁 Project Structure

```
.
├── app.py              # Main Streamlit application
├── requirements.txt    # Python dependencies
├── README.md
├── .gitignore
└── queueless.db        # SQLite database (auto-generated, not committed)
```

## 🧪 Typical Workflow

1. Open **Get Token** and generate a few tokens.
2. Open **Staff Console**, then **Call Next** on an open counter. The token moves to *serving*.
3. Click **Complete** to mark it as served.
4. Check **Dashboard** and **Analytics** to see the updated numbers.

## ⚠️ Known Limitations

- **Queue Vision** uses simple edge/contour detection, so counts are approximate. It is meant as a demo.
- Counter open/closed status is stored per browser session, while tokens are stored in the database.
- The app has no user login, so it is not ready for public deployment as-is.

## 🔮 Future Improvements

- [ ] Replace the contour counter with **YOLO** person detection (`ultralytics`)
